WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.136
Every insertion or deletion in a linked

00:00:03.209 --> 00:00:05.390
list changes one or two links.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:07.426
Three rules keep that safe.

00:00:07.426 --> 00:00:09.462
One: don't break the chain.

00:00:09.535 --> 00:00:12.443
If the only reference to the rest of the

00:00:12.516 --> 00:00:15.352
list is overwritten before it is saved

00:00:15.352 --> 00:00:17.533
the rest of the list is lost.

00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:20.733
Java's garbage collector reclaims it; in C

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:21.460
it leaks.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.320
Two: consider the special cases.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.143
The empty list, a list of one node

00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:29.104
changing the first node

00:00:29.104 --> 00:00:30.986
changing the last node

00:00:30.986 --> 00:00:33.810
and a position that does not exist.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.443
Three: draw a picture first.

00:00:36.443 --> 00:00:39.949
Boxes for the nodes, arrows for the links

00:00:40.031 --> 00:00:42.803
and number the arrows in the order

00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:44.760
your code changes them.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.335
Insert at the beginning.

00:00:47.335 --> 00:00:50.909
Make a newNode, here holding 34.

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:53.901
Step one: point the newNode at the

00:00:53.984 --> 00:00:55.563
current first node

00:00:55.563 --> 00:00:58.140
newNode.next equals head.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:00:59.217
Step two

00:00:59.217 --> 00:01:02.205
make the head point at the newNode

00:01:02.205 --> 00:01:03.981
head equals newNode.

00:01:03.981 --> 00:01:07.050
34 is now the first element.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.715
Two link changes

00:01:08.715 --> 00:01:12.040
whatever the length of the list: O(1).

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.515
It also works on an empty list.

00:01:14.515 --> 00:01:15.597
Head is null

00:01:15.597 --> 00:01:19.000
so the newNode.next simply becomes null.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.123
The order matters.

00:01:21.123 --> 00:01:24.980
Suppose we write head equals newNode first.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.687
The head was the only reference

00:01:27.763 --> 00:01:29.815
to the old list, so 12

00:01:29.815 --> 00:01:33.084
52 and 16 are now unreachable.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:36.200
Then newNode.next equals head makes

00:01:36.276 --> 00:01:38.481
the newNode point to itself.

00:01:38.481 --> 00:01:41.065
We are left with a one-node loop

00:01:41.065 --> 00:01:43.650
and the rest of the list is gone.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:47.153
Insert at the end has two cases.

00:01:47.153 --> 00:01:49.802
Case one: the list is empty

00:01:49.802 --> 00:01:52.177
so there is no last node.

00:01:52.177 --> 00:01:55.100
The newNode, becomes the head

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.470
and we return.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:00.198
insertLast(34) on an empty

00:02:00.287 --> 00:02:04.020
list gives the one-node list, 34.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.489
Case two: the list is not empty.

00:02:07.489 --> 00:02:10.648
Here it holds 34, 12

00:02:10.648 --> 00:02:14.529
52 and 16, and we insertLast

00:02:14.529 --> 00:02:15.251
12.

00:02:15.251 --> 00:02:17.779
current starts at the head.

00:02:17.779 --> 00:02:21.028
While current.next is not null

00:02:21.028 --> 00:02:25.000
current moves on: to 52, to 16.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:27.773
16's next is null

00:02:27.773 --> 00:02:31.564
so the loop stops on the last node.

00:02:31.564 --> 00:02:35.567
Now current.next equals newNode

00:02:35.567 --> 00:02:39.885
the old last node points to the new one.

00:02:39.885 --> 00:02:44.519
The list is 34, 12, 52

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:46.310
16, 12.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.888
The walk visits every node

00:02:48.888 --> 00:02:51.034
so insertLast is O(n).

00:02:51.117 --> 00:02:53.923
Keeping an extra tail reference to

00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:56.647
the last node makes it O(1)

00:02:56.647 --> 00:02:58.380
part 4 does that.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.586
Now insert after a given node.

00:03:01.586 --> 00:03:04.999
Call it target: here the first 12.

00:03:04.999 --> 00:03:07.800
The newNode holds 99.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:08.772
First

00:03:08.772 --> 00:03:13.123
point the newNode at target's successor

00:03:13.123 --> 00:03:17.474
newNode.next equals target.next.

00:03:17.474 --> 00:03:21.100
Then point target at the newNode

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.519
target.next equals newNode.

00:03:24.519 --> 00:03:28.663
The chain now runs 34, 12

00:03:28.663 --> 00:03:32.910
99, 52, 16, 12.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:34.481
Now predict.

00:03:34.563 --> 00:03:36.710
What goes wrong if the two

00:03:36.792 --> 00:03:38.278
lines are swapped

00:03:38.361 --> 00:03:40.837
so that target.next equals

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.653
newNode comes first?

00:03:42.653 --> 00:03:45.130
Pause the video, and draw it.

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:49.977
Here is the bug.

00:03:49.977 --> 00:03:53.066
After target.next equals newNode

00:03:53.146 --> 00:03:55.126
the only reference to the

00:03:55.205 --> 00:03:57.819
old successor is gone: 52

00:03:57.819 --> 00:04:00.670
16 and 12 are unreachable.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:04.531
Then newNode.next equals target.next

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:07.780
reads the link we just changed

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.764
so the newNode points to itself.

00:04:10.764 --> 00:04:14.100
The list now ends in a one-node loop.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.029
Any traversal then runs forever

00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:19.849
going round and round 99.

00:04:19.849 --> 00:04:23.154
So: first link the newNode to the rest

00:04:23.154 --> 00:04:25.410
then redirect the old link.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:29.405
The slides' insert method uses a dummy head

00:04:29.405 --> 00:04:31.889
head is a node with no element

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:35.149
and the first real node is head.next.

00:04:35.149 --> 00:04:38.176
insert takes a value and a position p

00:04:38.176 --> 00:04:41.125
and makes the value the p-th element.

00:04:41.125 --> 00:04:44.230
The dummy head counts as position zero.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.593
Insert(99, 3).

00:04:47.593 --> 00:04:49.140
Make the newNode

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.315
and start prevNode at the dummy head.

00:04:52.315 --> 00:04:55.898
The loop moves p minus one steps, here two

00:04:55.898 --> 00:04:58.503
to 34, then to 12

00:04:58.503 --> 00:05:00.620
the node at position two.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:03.463
Then the same two links as before

00:05:03.537 --> 00:05:05.517
the newNode points to

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:06.837
prevNode's successor

00:05:06.837 --> 00:05:09.623
and prevNode points to the newNode.

00:05:09.623 --> 00:05:12.043
Because prevNode always exists

00:05:12.043 --> 00:05:14.390
at worst the dummy head itself

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:17.617
there is no special case for an empty list

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:19.890
or for inserting at the front.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:23.103
The loop runs min(p - 1,

00:05:23.191 --> 00:05:25.736
size) times

00:05:25.736 --> 00:05:28.281
and the linking is O(1).

00:05:28.281 --> 00:05:31.967
So the best case, inserting at the front

00:05:31.967 --> 00:05:33.108
is O(1).

00:05:33.108 --> 00:05:36.443
The worst case, inserting at the end

00:05:36.443 --> 00:05:37.409
is O(n).

00:05:37.409 --> 00:05:40.656
And for a position chosen at random

00:05:40.656 --> 00:05:44.430
about n/2 steps: O(n) on average.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:47.954
If p is larger than the size plus one

00:05:47.954 --> 00:05:50.710
the loop stops at the last node

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:52.297
and x is appended.

00:05:52.297 --> 00:05:55.972
If p is one or less, the loop does not run

00:05:55.972 --> 00:05:58.060
and x goes to the front.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:05:59.770
Now deletion.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:00.987
deleteFirst()

00:06:00.987 --> 00:06:04.899
on an empty list there is nothing to delete

00:06:04.899 --> 00:06:06.638
so it returns null.

00:06:06.638 --> 00:06:10.289
Otherwise, temp remembers the first node

00:06:10.289 --> 00:06:13.680
and the head moves to the second node

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.027
head = head.next.

00:06:16.027 --> 00:06:18.200
The method returns temp.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:20.394
Once the caller drops it

00:06:20.394 --> 00:06:22.975
the old first node is unreachable

00:06:22.975 --> 00:06:26.073
and Java's garbage collector reclaims it.

00:06:26.073 --> 00:06:27.990
deleteFirst() is O(1).

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:29.780
deleteLast().

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:33.714
If the list is empty, it returns null

00:06:33.714 --> 00:06:36.135
and if it has one node

00:06:36.135 --> 00:06:38.556
the list becomes empty.

00:06:38.556 --> 00:06:42.993
Otherwise, walk to the second-to-last node

00:06:42.993 --> 00:06:45.515
curr starts at the head

00:06:45.616 --> 00:06:48.844
and the loop runs while

00:06:48.944 --> 00:06:51.567
curr.next.next is not null.

00:06:51.567 --> 00:06:52.777
12: no.

00:06:52.777 --> 00:06:54.290
52: no.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:58.458
At 16, curr.next.next is null

00:06:58.458 --> 00:07:01.655
so curr is the second-to-last node.

00:07:01.655 --> 00:07:04.318
Temp remembers the last node

00:07:04.318 --> 00:07:08.136
and curr.next equals null cuts it off.

00:07:08.136 --> 00:07:10.800
16 is now the last node.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:13.565
A singly linked list has no way

00:07:13.643 --> 00:07:15.513
back from the last node

00:07:15.591 --> 00:07:17.851
so even with a tail reference

00:07:17.929 --> 00:07:20.110
this needs the walk: O(n).

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:24.192
To delete the node after a given node, prev

00:07:24.192 --> 00:07:27.535
here the first 12, make prev skip it

00:07:27.535 --> 00:07:31.117
prev.next equals prev.next.next.

00:07:31.196 --> 00:07:33.743
The check first makes sure there

00:07:33.823 --> 00:07:36.290
is a node after prev to delete.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:39.272
The bypassed node, 52

00:07:39.272 --> 00:07:42.903
is unreachable, and the list is 12

00:07:42.903 --> 00:07:43.720
16.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:45.816
With a dummy head

00:07:45.816 --> 00:07:49.100
delete at position p is the same idea

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:52.468
walk prevNode to position p minus one

00:07:52.468 --> 00:07:54.741
then bypass its successor.

00:07:54.741 --> 00:07:57.267
If p is larger than the size

00:07:57.267 --> 00:07:59.372
the target node is null

00:07:59.372 --> 00:08:01.140
and nothing happens.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:05.168
The running time is the same as for insert

00:08:05.168 --> 00:08:06.879
best case O(1)

00:08:06.879 --> 00:08:09.530
worst and average case O(n).

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:13.409
Here is the dummy head list at work.

00:08:13.409 --> 00:08:17.257
Inserting 34, 12, 52

00:08:17.257 --> 00:08:19.090
16 and 12

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:21.564
each at the next position

00:08:21.564 --> 00:08:23.579
prints them in order.

00:08:23.579 --> 00:08:25.870
find(52) is 3.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.763
insert(99, 3) puts it third.

00:08:29.763 --> 00:08:33.559
insert(7, 100) is past the end

00:08:33.559 --> 00:08:35.415
so 7 is appended.

00:08:35.415 --> 00:08:38.620
insert(1, 0) goes to the front.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:42.200
delete(1) removes the first element.

00:08:42.287 --> 00:08:44.644
delete(find(52))

00:08:44.731 --> 00:08:46.302
removes 52.

00:08:46.302 --> 00:08:50.056
delete(100) finds no such position

00:08:50.056 --> 00:08:51.802
so nothing happens.

00:08:51.802 --> 00:08:55.642
And makeEmpty leaves the dummy head alone

00:08:55.642 --> 00:08:57.650
the list prints empty.

00:08:58.230 --> 00:08:59.495
And in an array?

00:08:59.495 --> 00:09:02.498
If the array is full, it cannot grow.

00:09:02.498 --> 00:09:03.209
Instead

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:06.213
add makes a new array twice as large

00:09:06.213 --> 00:09:08.426
copies the elements across

00:09:08.426 --> 00:09:11.350
and from then on uses the new array.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:13.938
Adding 1 to 17

00:09:13.938 --> 00:09:16.549
starting from capacity 1

00:09:16.549 --> 00:09:20.373
the array fills at sizes 1, 2, 4

00:09:20.373 --> 00:09:22.144
8 and 16

00:09:22.238 --> 00:09:24.849
and ends with size 17

00:09:24.942 --> 00:09:27.180
and capacity 32.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:29.588
31 copies in total

00:09:29.588 --> 00:09:32.028
fewer than 2 times 17.

00:09:32.028 --> 00:09:35.382
Because the array doubles, copies are rare

00:09:35.382 --> 00:09:38.202
and each add is O(1) on average.

00:09:38.202 --> 00:09:40.718
Java's ArrayList works this way

00:09:40.718 --> 00:09:43.233
growing by 1.5 times.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:45.520
But inserting at the front of

00:09:45.596 --> 00:09:47.502
an array is still O(n)

00:09:47.502 --> 00:09:50.170
that is what linked lists are for.

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:51.669
To sum up.

00:09:51.669 --> 00:09:54.240
1: don't break the chain.

00:09:54.332 --> 00:09:56.536
Link the new node to the

00:09:56.628 --> 00:09:58.741
rest of the list first

00:09:58.741 --> 00:10:02.139
and only then redirect the old link.

00:10:02.139 --> 00:10:04.894
2: inserting or deleting at

00:10:04.986 --> 00:10:08.109
the front is O(1); at the end

00:10:08.109 --> 00:10:12.150
or at a position, the walk makes it O(n).

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:15.180
3: a dummy head turns the front

00:10:15.256 --> 00:10:17.247
into an ordinary position

00:10:17.247 --> 00:10:19.620
so there are no special cases.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:20.948
Try this.

00:10:20.948 --> 00:10:24.966
Start from an empty dummy head list

00:10:24.966 --> 00:10:28.007
and run insert(4, 1)

00:10:28.007 --> 00:10:32.351
insert(8, 2), insert(6, 2)

00:10:32.351 --> 00:10:36.369
insert(2, 1), and delete(3).

00:10:36.369 --> 00:10:39.410
What does printList print?

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:40.876
In part 3

00:10:40.876 --> 00:10:43.664
we put it all into one Java class

00:10:43.664 --> 00:10:46.293
and solve two classic exercises

00:10:46.293 --> 00:10:49.400
the middle node, and reversing a list.

